.TH SVC 8
.SH NAME
svc \- the services init supervises
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B svc
.br
.B svc restart
.I name
.br
.B svc stop
.I name
.br
.B svc idle
.I name seconds
.br
.B svc add
.RB [ \-p
.I port
.RB [ \-i
.IR seconds ]]
.RB [ \-w
.IR port ]
.RB [ \-b ]
.I name path
.RB [ always | on-failure | never ]
.RI [ arg ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B svc
asks
.BR init (8)
(port
.BR init )
about the services it supervises. Without arguments it lists them: name,
state
.RB ( running ,
.BR waiting ,
.BR "GIVEN UP" ,
.BR stopped ,
.BR idle ),
pid, restarts, last exit status, policy, uptime, memory and its limit,
starts, and for a service started on demand the port that starts it and
its idle time. Everything else is for root.
.TP
.B restart
Stop it if it runs, start it again (also after init gave up on it).
.TP
.B stop
Stop it for good.
.TP
.B idle
An on-demand service stops after
.I seconds
unused (0: never).
.TP
.B add
Supervise the program
.I path
from the disk as
.IR name ,
with its arguments and a policy: start it again
.B always
(the default),
.B on-failure
(a status other than 0), or
.BR never .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-p " port"
Start it on demand, when
.I port
is looked up, not at once.
.TP
.BI \-i " seconds"
With
.BR \-p :
its idle time.
.TP
.BI \-w " port"
Watch it: init pings
.I port
and ends the server if it stops answering (30 s), so it is started
again.
.TP
.B \-b
Background work: on E-cores where the processor has them.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
svc
svc restart sshd
svc add \-w stuck hung /bin/crash never hang stuck
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR init (8),
.BR ps (1),
.BR kill (1)
.SH BUGS
Services added with
.B svc add
are forgotten at the next start: init's own table is built into it.
